@page "/"
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Authorization

<PageTitle>Dynamic Book Tracker</PageTitle>

<div class="home-shell">
    <section class="hero-panel">
        <div class="hero-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow">Realtime demo experience</p>
            <h1>Bring a cosy bookshop to life with live updates.</h1>
            <p class="hero-lead">
                Dynamic Book Tracker shows how Web PubSub can keep a borrowing experience feeling instant, collaborative, and presentation-ready.
                Borrow a title, return it, or notify a watcher and the UI responds in the moment.
            </p>

            <div class="hero-actions">
                <AuthorizeView>
                    <Authorized>
                        <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/books">Open the live library</a>
                        <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-lg" href="/.auth/logout">Sign out</a>
                    </Authorized>
                    <NotAuthorized>
                        <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/.auth/login/aad">Log in to start the demo</a>
                        <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-lg" href="/books">Preview the library route</a>
                    </NotAuthorized>
                </AuthorizeView>
            </div>

            <AuthorizeView>
                <Authorized>
                    <div class="guest-note">
                        <strong>Signed in:</strong> Head to the live library to demo borrowing, watch notifications, and real-time status changes.
                    </div>
                </Authorized>
                <NotAuthorized>
                    <div class="guest-note">
                        <strong>Best demo flow:</strong> Start here, log in, then open the library and trigger a book change from another client to show instant updates.
                    </div>
                </NotAuthorized>
            </AuthorizeView>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-card card-surface">
            <h2>Why this works well in a demo</h2>
            <ul class="feature-list">
                <li><span class="feature-badge">Live</span> Book availability refreshes without manual reloading.</li>
                <li><span class="feature-badge">Clear</span> Large contrast-rich controls stay readable on a projector.</li>
                <li><span class="feature-badge">Simple</span> The story is easy to narrate from sign-in to live updates.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="stat-grid">
                <div>
                    <span class="stat-label">Message flow</span>
                    <strong>Web PubSub driven</strong>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <span class="stat-label">Best suited for</span>
                    <strong>Responsive UI demos</strong>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <span class="stat-label">Primary interaction</span>
                    <strong>Borrow, return, notify</strong>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <span class="stat-label">Mood</span>
                    <strong>Warm, legible, modern</strong>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="info-grid">
        <div class="card-surface info-card">
            <h2>How to tell the story</h2>
            <p>Open the library, change a book's availability, and let the audience see the page update itself as the message arrives.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-surface info-card">
            <h2>What the audience notices</h2>
            <p>The interface stays calm and readable while still making every live change obvious enough to feel satisfying.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-surface info-card">
            <h2>What the code demonstrates</h2>
            <p>A lightweight UI can still feel responsive, polished, and realtime when messaging is wired cleanly into the client.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
